a lot can change in 10 years and a lot  has changed since 2009  in October of that year gearbox Studios  released Borderlands by combining the  progression of Diablo with the  satisfaction of a first-person shooter  it established the loot shooter as a  viable genre it set the stage for  staples like destiny the division and  warframe to break on to the scene  Borderlands 2 made its predecessor look  like a mere proof-of-concept the sequel  took everything that made the first game  great and amplified it fleshing out  characters weapons and progression  systems to add longevity to a proven  formula looking back on the hundreds of  hours I spent with Maya Salvador and  crew I still remember thinking how could  someone possibly improve on this which  brings us to Borderlands 3 I have had to  use a cliche a roller coaster experience  with this game in its early hours I was  critical Borderlands 3 opens with a  whimper front-loading its most boring  environments and my least favorite NPCs  he also didn't help that in the seven  years since Borderlands 2 my  appreciation for gearboxes humor has  significantly waned  suffice it to say I was apprehensive  moving forward by the time I reached  Eden 6 the fourth planet in Borderlands  threes larger galaxy I was disappointed  the formula felt dated it felt as if  gearbox had run out of ideas opting to  keep the great wheel spinning rather  than try to reinvent it because of this  it was still fun don't get me wrong but  some of my favorite games this year from  Metro Exodus to Gears 5 have drastically  questioned their status quo they chose  to reshape what didn't technically need  reshaping I was hoping with its  seven-year gap that gearbox might  somehow do the same I finished  Borderlands 3s campaign and resigned  myself to the fact that it hadn't but  then I kept playing and all of a sudden  over the course of the next two hours it  all came into focus  despite its garish color palette and  kaleidoscopic display of explosions  Borderlands 3 is very much a slow burn  it's only in its later hours the  hallowed endgame that it shows its true  work and now I'm absolutely hooked  there are various threads you can follow  once you've beaten the campaign the most  obvious is true vault hunter mode a  carryover from Borderlands 2 that allows  you to restart the story at your current  rank essentially its Borderlands threes  new game+ you could also try  slaughterhouse 3000 a wave defense mode  that will undoubtedly help you reach the  level 50 cap and improve your Guardian  rank speaking of which Guardian rank  acts as a second layer of character  progression replacing the badass ranks  of Borderlands 2 you'll earn Guardian  ranks at a fairly steady pace allowing  you to upgrade traits such as grenade  damage or fight for your life survival  time but while true vault hunter  Guardian ranks and the slaughterhouse  all do reward you for your time its  Borderlands threes new endgame options  that really make it shine my personal  favorite is mayhem mode essentially this  allows you to 100% the campaign while  also improving your character this means  you can finish those side quests you  miss or grab all of those collectables  you've previously ignored without  wasting your time  mayhem upscales all of these objectives  giving you commensurate loot and  experience points for your character as  someone who spent dozens of hours  mopping a Borderlands two side quests  that were dozens of ranks below in my  character level I love this most of  Borderlands threes best missions are  outside of the main narrative and mayhem  is an elegant way to reward players  hesitant to restart the campaign what's  more there are three tiers of mayhem  mode with the second and third offering  better rewards for tougher fights and  more random challenges gearbox does need  to do some balancing with these tiers  but the fact that they exist and offer  an alternative to true vault hunter  tells me that gearbox recognized one of  Borderlands twos few redundancies and  none of the system mentioned proving  grounds which in conjunction with mayhem  mode make for some of the most rewarding  border lancing I've experienced these  six trials act as many dungeons so to  speak offering quick-fire ways to earn  experience and improve  phoo they're also endlessly replayable  and conducive to speedruns so they're a  great place for you to test your endgame  characters but keep improving them in  the process  all these endgame avenues have me  convinced that gearbox did in fact  improve the borderlands formula it just  did so in surprisingly subtle ways it  figured out how best to respect players  time I can't overstate how important  that has become today we have so many  games as service to choose from that  it's actually impossible to see the  breath of everyone more developers are  competing for our time than ever before  we have to carefully consider which  titles will really dig into the concept  of an endgame is so important because  when done well it rewards that loyalty  Bioware's anthem is struggling in large  part because it has not figured this out  warframe a game that's hugely successful  in its own right doesn't really have an  endgame a large number of veteran  players are looking for ways to use  their hard-earned rewards but as of now  they're sort of grasping at straws this  all reminds me of a quote from Moga mu a  youtuber that built his channel largely  around warframe a few years ago he  described the feeling that resonated  with many longtime players he said we're  all dressed up with nowhere to go  water lines 3 is not revolutionary in  large part it gives Borderlands fans  more of what they want and that's  completely fine it's not what I was  really hoping for but it's what I  expected and it makes sense but I wasn't  expecting to find so much depth in  Borderlands threes end game it shows to  me that gearbox did in some way want to  tweak the larger Borderlands formula it  knows the value of respecting dedicated  players time and it's here in its later  hours when it shows it's real worth it  tells you to dress up yes but then it  gives you somewhere to go
 

